# 2. BUSINESS MODEL – MUST SUPPORT ALL THREE SCENARIOS

The entire application must be designed around three execution models.

SCENARIO A – DIRECT AUDITOR ASSIGNMENT

Example:

JaySoftShine receives a Physical Verification project from an MNC.

Client rate: ₹1,500 per manday.

Auditor rate: ₹1,000 per manday.

Margin: ₹500 per manday.

The project has 2 locations and 2 individual auditors.

The administrator creates a project, adds two locations and directly allocates one auditor to each location.

The system must calculate:
- Client billing.
- Auditor payable.
- Actual approved mandays.
- Gross margin.
- Other expenses.
- Net project profit.

SCENARIO B – COMPLETE VENDOR OUTSOURCING

Example:

JaySoftShine receives a 100-location assignment.

Client rate: ₹1,500 per manday.

Vendor rate: ₹1,200 per manday.

Vendor manages all 100 locations and may allocate its own auditors or sub-vendors.

JaySoftShine monitors progress, attendance, reports, billing, vendor bills, payment and project profitability.

SCENARIO C – HYBRID EXECUTION

Example:

100 locations:
- 20 locations directly allocated to individual auditors.
- 80 locations allocated to Vendor XYZ.

Both execution types must exist under one parent project.

The system must support multiple vendors, sub-vendors, individual auditors and different commercial rates in the same project.

CRITICAL BUSINESS RULE:
The client billing rate, vendor rate and auditor rate must be independently maintained. External users must never see JaySoftShine's client rates, overall business margin or another vendor's commercial information.

